WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.780 --> 00:00:01.360
Hi all.

00:00:01.360 --> 00:00:02.950
My name's Adam Haar Horowitz.

00:00:02.950 --> 00:00:08.020
I'm a PhD student in the Media
Lab in the fluid interfaces

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:08.590
group.

00:00:08.590 --> 00:00:10.720
And I'm really
happy to be joined

00:00:10.720 --> 00:00:14.952
by Agnieszka Kurant for
casts three questions series.

00:00:14.952 --> 00:00:17.410
And we're going to be speaking
through a couple of projects

00:00:17.410 --> 00:00:20.230
that I Agnieszka did in her
visiting artist residency

00:00:20.230 --> 00:00:21.570
and one that we did together.

00:00:21.570 --> 00:00:24.070
Agnieszka, do you want to start
just by introducing yourself

00:00:24.070 --> 00:00:25.740
to people?

00:00:25.740 --> 00:00:27.780
Hello everyone, and
thank you so much

00:00:27.780 --> 00:00:31.320
to Cast for hosting this talk.

00:00:31.320 --> 00:00:32.640
My name is Agnieszka Kurant.

00:00:32.640 --> 00:00:34.210
I'm a visual artist.

00:00:34.210 --> 00:00:41.310
And I was a visiting artist
at MIT Cast from 2016 to 2018.

00:00:41.310 --> 00:00:45.360
Agnieszka, I know that in past
works like production line

00:00:45.360 --> 00:00:47.850
or the end of the
signature for you,

00:00:47.850 --> 00:00:49.670
automation, collective
intelligence,

00:00:49.670 --> 00:00:52.510
and shared authorship have
been big themes in your work.

00:00:52.510 --> 00:00:54.450
And so I just thought
we could start

00:00:54.450 --> 00:00:57.490
with how those were
points of departure

00:00:57.490 --> 00:01:00.480
for your collaboration
with Boris Katz

00:01:00.480 --> 00:01:01.890
and that info group at MIT.

00:01:05.280 --> 00:01:09.060
During my residency at
MIT, I was collaborating

00:01:09.060 --> 00:01:18.330
with the CSAIL and artificial
intelligence lab of Boris Katz.

00:01:18.330 --> 00:01:23.540
And we were working together
with Boris' fantastic

00:01:23.540 --> 00:01:29.520
PhD and postdoc students,
Andrei Barbu and David Mayo,

00:01:29.520 --> 00:01:33.930
on a project that
stemmed from my earlier

00:01:33.930 --> 00:01:37.750
research and projects
that I've done before.

00:01:37.750 --> 00:01:42.510
So I would like to share
with you a couple of images.

00:01:42.510 --> 00:01:44.970
So I've been interested
in the phenomenon

00:01:44.970 --> 00:01:48.060
of collective
intelligence that can

00:01:48.060 --> 00:01:53.870
be perceived in both nature and
culture for a very long time.

00:01:53.870 --> 00:01:58.050
And this, for example,
is one of my projects

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:03.000
where I outsource or
crowdsource my artworks

00:02:03.000 --> 00:02:07.540
to colonies of living termites.

00:02:07.540 --> 00:02:12.480
And together with entomologists
from University of Florida,

00:02:12.480 --> 00:02:15.270
we gave termite
colonies very vividly

00:02:15.270 --> 00:02:18.510
colored sands, gold,
and broken crystals.

00:02:18.510 --> 00:02:21.480
And each colony built
one of these mounds.

00:02:21.480 --> 00:02:25.110
So this here what you
can see is a series

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.170
of works called the
end of signature,

00:02:28.170 --> 00:02:32.730
where I collect signatures
of thousands of people

00:02:32.730 --> 00:02:37.200
of various communities of social
movements of people supporting

00:02:37.200 --> 00:02:39.510
one cause, such
as against climate

00:02:39.510 --> 00:02:43.140
change, and different
kind of activist groups,

00:02:43.140 --> 00:02:46.610
but also members of
the worker's unions.

00:02:46.610 --> 00:02:54.520
And in this series I work each
time with a computer scientist

00:02:54.520 --> 00:02:57.630
or programmer to kind of morph--

00:02:57.630 --> 00:02:59.970
aggregate together thousands
of these signatures

00:02:59.970 --> 00:03:03.930
and create one single
collective signature

00:03:03.930 --> 00:03:06.090
that represents kind of
collective personality

00:03:06.090 --> 00:03:07.470
of a given community.

00:03:07.470 --> 00:03:10.680
This is a facade of
the Guggenheim Museum

00:03:10.680 --> 00:03:14.140
where we collected
signatures of the visitors.

00:03:14.140 --> 00:03:17.070
This is on the facade of
the Cleveland Museum of Art,

00:03:17.070 --> 00:03:19.230
where these are
all the workers--

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:21.440
all the employees
of this museum.

00:03:21.440 --> 00:03:24.360
That's contract workers from
the members of the board

00:03:24.360 --> 00:03:29.970
to the guards and
the cleaning ladies.

00:03:29.970 --> 00:03:32.340
This is a collective
signature of all the people

00:03:32.340 --> 00:03:38.470
living in this group of
buildings in this council

00:03:38.470 --> 00:03:39.860
project.

00:03:39.860 --> 00:03:43.230
The themes with the end of--
signature end production line

00:03:43.230 --> 00:03:46.410
in terms of hybrid authorship
and hybrid production

00:03:46.410 --> 00:03:51.240
and materialization of a
collective intelligence--

00:03:51.240 --> 00:03:54.200
I think it's a good segue into
the animal internet project,

00:03:54.200 --> 00:03:57.270
if you could take
people there, perhaps.

00:03:57.270 --> 00:03:58.590
Yes, absolutely.

00:03:58.590 --> 00:04:02.440
This is the image from,
like, a work in progress

00:04:02.440 --> 00:04:05.590
of the project done
at Boris Katz's

00:04:05.590 --> 00:04:08.910
lab with his PhD and
postdoc students.

00:04:08.910 --> 00:04:11.970
So it was Boris who
collected thousands

00:04:11.970 --> 00:04:17.490
of self portraits taken by
workers on Amazon Mechanical

00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:18.060
Turk.

00:04:18.060 --> 00:04:22.500
And we created a program that
aggregated all these portraits,

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.860
one line of pixels
from each photograph

00:04:25.860 --> 00:04:29.580
to create this composite,
collective portrait

00:04:29.580 --> 00:04:31.170
of this new working class.

00:04:31.170 --> 00:04:33.900
Then we created
the 3D sculptures

00:04:33.900 --> 00:04:37.857
that were a 3D cut resin
with copper and nickel plated

00:04:37.857 --> 00:04:39.690
that were these kind
of collective portraits

00:04:39.690 --> 00:04:40.837
of these working class.

00:04:40.837 --> 00:04:43.170
This was a point of departure
for our project for animal

00:04:43.170 --> 00:04:44.720
internet.

00:04:44.720 --> 00:04:51.160
Animal internet, it was based
on my interest in how today

00:04:51.160 --> 00:04:54.890
social energies and society are
being mined in a similar way

00:04:54.890 --> 00:04:59.030
to oil and gas, how
expressions of our joy,

00:04:59.030 --> 00:05:02.980
anger, frustration, and
enthusiam, happiness,

00:05:02.980 --> 00:05:09.070
are constantly being monitored
by corporations and harvested.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:13.030
And how can we ourselves
take advantage of this--

00:05:13.030 --> 00:05:15.760
of our own social
capital and maybe

00:05:15.760 --> 00:05:17.560
use it for other
type of projects?

00:05:17.560 --> 00:05:21.010
So I looked at this phenomenon
called animal internet that

00:05:21.010 --> 00:05:24.880
was described very interestingly
in a book by Alexander Pschera

00:05:24.880 --> 00:05:27.670
about three years ago, a
book of the same title.

00:05:27.670 --> 00:05:29.500
And he described
this phenomenon where

00:05:29.500 --> 00:05:34.240
people start perceiving nature
and start being with nature

00:05:34.240 --> 00:05:37.190
in various different ways.

00:05:37.190 --> 00:05:40.630
For example, there are
animals that have chips

00:05:40.630 --> 00:05:43.690
under their skin and they are
being tracked on animal tracker

00:05:43.690 --> 00:05:45.370
apps around the world.

00:05:45.370 --> 00:05:49.090
There's about 150,000
of these animals.

00:05:49.090 --> 00:05:52.420
And at the same time, a lot
of scientific institutes

00:05:52.420 --> 00:05:55.450
install web cams and
jungles, or in the North

00:05:55.450 --> 00:06:02.420
Pole to watch completely wild
and completely unaware animals.

00:06:02.420 --> 00:06:06.100
And these broadcasts are being
transmitted online and people

00:06:06.100 --> 00:06:08.020
start following these animals.

00:06:08.020 --> 00:06:12.040
These animals are being given
Facebook pages, fan pages,

00:06:12.040 --> 00:06:13.090
names.

00:06:13.090 --> 00:06:18.040
So there's this kind
of hybrid situation

00:06:18.040 --> 00:06:20.740
of these animals being
completely wild and unaware.

00:06:20.740 --> 00:06:23.230
And meanwhile, they
become celebrities online

00:06:23.230 --> 00:06:24.670
and people follow that.

00:06:24.670 --> 00:06:29.410
So our project was Adam and
our other collaborators.

00:06:29.410 --> 00:06:33.940
We used broadcasts from two
webcams, two real webcams,

00:06:33.940 --> 00:06:35.020
that you can see here.

00:06:35.020 --> 00:06:37.360
One is polar bear
in the North Pole

00:06:37.360 --> 00:06:42.430
and one is a wild tiger
in a jungle in Thailand.

00:06:42.430 --> 00:06:44.410
And we juxtaposed
these two broadcasts

00:06:44.410 --> 00:06:49.310
with two other broadcasts
that we created from scratch

00:06:49.310 --> 00:06:51.920
with our group of
artists and engineers.

00:06:51.920 --> 00:06:59.050
And so we basically created two
fictional animatronic animals.

00:06:59.050 --> 00:07:01.750
One is an animal in
the upper left corner.

00:07:01.750 --> 00:07:05.230
You can see an image of
kind of furry animal,

00:07:05.230 --> 00:07:08.800
just wanted to say that we
didn't-- that all the fur used

00:07:08.800 --> 00:07:10.440
in this project was artificial.

00:07:10.440 --> 00:07:12.380
No animals were harmed.

00:07:12.380 --> 00:07:16.000
So we used this
artificial fur to hide

00:07:16.000 --> 00:07:17.320
an animatronic mechanism.

00:07:17.320 --> 00:07:22.030
And we included real plants
and stones and fragments

00:07:22.030 --> 00:07:26.050
of wood in, like, a terrarium.

00:07:26.050 --> 00:07:29.590
This animal, this
artificial animal, was--

00:07:29.590 --> 00:07:33.040
its movements were
powered by inputs

00:07:33.040 --> 00:07:41.860
from thousands of people who
were sending us information

00:07:41.860 --> 00:07:44.170
on Amazon Mechanical
Turk about how

00:07:44.170 --> 00:07:46.740
they feel at any given moment.

00:07:46.740 --> 00:07:49.570
These were aggregated, and
Adam will tell a bit more

00:07:49.570 --> 00:07:53.730
in a couple of minutes about the
technical side of this project.

00:07:53.730 --> 00:07:59.380
But just to mention the other
bottom right corner, camera,

00:07:59.380 --> 00:08:02.710
this broadcast was
from the webcam

00:08:02.710 --> 00:08:07.090
that we also kind of built from
scratch in another terrarium.

00:08:07.090 --> 00:08:10.090
And these are robotic,
animatronic gerbils.

00:08:10.090 --> 00:08:14.290
And this swarm of gerbles was
kind of-- their movement was

00:08:14.290 --> 00:08:18.880
powered by, well, a program
that was parsing Twitter feeds

00:08:18.880 --> 00:08:19.930
for protest--

00:08:19.930 --> 00:08:22.550
of members of protest
movements around the world.

00:08:22.550 --> 00:08:27.370
And this was then analyzed in an
artificial society model, which

00:08:27.370 --> 00:08:33.970
is an animation model of an
artificial, fictional society

00:08:33.970 --> 00:08:36.490
that our fantastic
engineers wrote.

00:08:36.490 --> 00:08:38.830
And so basically,
these for cameras

00:08:38.830 --> 00:08:45.850
were juxtaposing real animals
and artificial animals into one

00:08:45.850 --> 00:08:48.260
broadcast where--

00:08:48.260 --> 00:08:51.070
for the people who watched
these animals online,

00:08:51.070 --> 00:08:54.020
it doesn't really matter
whether the real designated

00:08:54.020 --> 00:08:56.590
nature of the real animals
exist, because anyway,

00:08:56.590 --> 00:08:59.860
they only see their images.

00:08:59.860 --> 00:09:02.050
And I will maybe
let Adam say more

00:09:02.050 --> 00:09:05.070
about the technical
side of this project.

00:09:05.070 --> 00:09:08.500
That was a content-loaded
and awesome summary.

00:09:08.500 --> 00:09:12.340
One piece that's fun for viewers
is how we created the project,

00:09:12.340 --> 00:09:16.755
because you initially approached
us to collaborate as part

00:09:16.755 --> 00:09:18.630
of the signature hack-in
[? Hacking Earth. ?]

00:09:18.630 --> 00:09:20.920
So the largest part
of this project

00:09:20.920 --> 00:09:24.520
was done in 24 hours, where you
stayed up with us until 3:00 AM

00:09:24.520 --> 00:09:29.440
and where we created the robotic
movements by stripping apart

00:09:29.440 --> 00:09:33.040
a bunch of robots and creating
our own strange hybrid organism

00:09:33.040 --> 00:09:35.320
from multiple
mechanical animals.

00:09:35.320 --> 00:09:39.000
And then also where Agnes coded
up a sugarscape model and then

00:09:39.000 --> 00:09:42.400
Ishan was scraping the
emotion using natural language

00:09:42.400 --> 00:09:45.720
processing from Twitter to
see the intensity of emotion

00:09:45.720 --> 00:09:48.630
related to tweets that had
the word protest in them.

00:09:48.630 --> 00:09:51.720
And that was then driving the
gerbil movement, and where

00:09:51.720 --> 00:09:55.398
then Owen was connecting with
Tim many different hex bugs

00:09:55.398 --> 00:09:56.940
so we could make
gerbils, which could

00:09:56.940 --> 00:09:59.580
communicate with each other
using radio frequency.

00:09:59.580 --> 00:10:03.330
And the whole time one thing
that was really fun for me

00:10:03.330 --> 00:10:06.630
is the discussions that we were
having were always tied back

00:10:06.630 --> 00:10:09.870
to neuroscience, tied back
to the idea of how humans are

00:10:09.870 --> 00:10:14.100
a hybrid organism, both in
terms of being cyborg hybrids

00:10:14.100 --> 00:10:15.810
and then also being
hybrids in terms

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:18.930
of our collective intelligence
on the internet changing

00:10:18.930 --> 00:10:20.430
our own self reflection.

00:10:20.430 --> 00:10:24.810
And so one really fun
thing is the production

00:10:24.810 --> 00:10:26.880
happening in such a hurry.

00:10:26.880 --> 00:10:29.790
And the other, I think,
is all of the resonance

00:10:29.790 --> 00:10:33.030
and themes between the
neuroscientific aspect of it

00:10:33.030 --> 00:10:35.280
and the human computer
interaction aspect of it,

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:37.260
and that tying into
the themes that you're

00:10:37.260 --> 00:10:39.750
speaking about in terms
of hybrid and collective

00:10:39.750 --> 00:10:40.510
intelligence.

00:10:40.510 --> 00:10:46.470
And for me, also, the
work has really continued.

00:10:46.470 --> 00:10:48.470
We were speaking about
this just a few days ago,

00:10:48.470 --> 00:10:51.820
but I'm starting a new project
with the support of CAST

00:10:51.820 --> 00:10:54.750
and [? Chem ?] at
MIT where, again,

00:10:54.750 --> 00:10:57.000
working with deconstructing
robotic animals

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:00.390
and reconstructing them and
connecting them to humans

00:11:00.390 --> 00:11:03.720
so that they can materialize
different sorts of changes

00:11:03.720 --> 00:11:04.460
in cognition.

00:11:04.460 --> 00:11:06.600
And right now the project
that I'm working on

00:11:06.600 --> 00:11:09.180
is a dream-activated
animals project,

00:11:09.180 --> 00:11:15.180
where we tie a sleep sensor to
somebody, leave them in a room,

00:11:15.180 --> 00:11:18.450
but it's full of robotic
animals that we've created.

00:11:18.450 --> 00:11:21.420
And when that person
enters a dream state, when

00:11:21.420 --> 00:11:24.420
they experience this
sea change in cognition,

00:11:24.420 --> 00:11:27.060
all those animals will
wake up and start walking.

00:11:27.060 --> 00:11:31.080
So their life only comes at the
onset of your unconsciousness.

00:11:31.080 --> 00:11:34.420
But it's really a
tie between that idea

00:11:34.420 --> 00:11:37.680
of how is it that we materialize
and mechanize and create

00:11:37.680 --> 00:11:40.440
these hybrid human
robot organisms

00:11:40.440 --> 00:11:43.680
and how is it that we can show
this energy in active form?

00:11:43.680 --> 00:11:46.050
And it was a real
continuation of this project.

00:11:46.050 --> 00:11:48.900
And so I was just
wondering also how

00:11:48.900 --> 00:11:52.110
maybe the sugarscape
model that we built

00:11:52.110 --> 00:11:56.243
or maybe the actual collective
mechanical hybrids, how

00:11:56.243 --> 00:11:57.660
any of those themes
have continued

00:11:57.660 --> 00:12:02.010
in your work going forward,
if any are still around?

00:12:02.010 --> 00:12:04.740
This is absolutely fascinating,
what you said, Adam.

00:12:04.740 --> 00:12:08.800
And I do believe that we will
continue working together

00:12:08.800 --> 00:12:09.300
very soon.

00:12:09.300 --> 00:12:09.840
Yeah.

00:12:09.840 --> 00:12:13.290
And so yes, I mean, there
is not enough time here

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:20.650
to explain all the
implications for this project.

00:12:20.650 --> 00:12:23.970
But of course, this hybrid
situations of, like,

00:12:23.970 --> 00:12:27.450
nature, culture overlap.

00:12:27.450 --> 00:12:29.580
And basically
the-- also the idea

00:12:29.580 --> 00:12:32.790
of the collapse of
the concept of self,

00:12:32.790 --> 00:12:36.990
I mean, the self as we knew
it no longer exists because it

00:12:36.990 --> 00:12:39.150
turns out that the
self is a polyphony,

00:12:39.150 --> 00:12:42.120
is an aggregate of
various kinds of agencies,

00:12:42.120 --> 00:12:46.110
from microbial to
algorithmic to the kind

00:12:46.110 --> 00:12:49.000
of collective intelligence as
humans being part of society.

00:12:49.000 --> 00:12:53.430
So all this has continued
to inform my practice.

00:12:53.430 --> 00:12:56.690
And I want to just
briefly show you, too,

00:12:56.690 --> 00:12:57.940
two works that I've recently--

00:12:57.940 --> 00:12:58.770
I love this.

00:12:58.770 --> 00:13:00.000
--completed.

00:13:00.000 --> 00:13:02.070
One of them is, I think,
particularly interesting

00:13:02.070 --> 00:13:05.908
because of what has happened
in the world since our project

00:13:05.908 --> 00:13:07.950
together, especially what's
happened in the world

00:13:07.950 --> 00:13:09.310
the past couple of weeks.

00:13:09.310 --> 00:13:13.320
So this project is called
collective Rorschach test.

00:13:13.320 --> 00:13:15.300
This is lenticular print.

00:13:15.300 --> 00:13:17.970
So here you can see
three different views

00:13:17.970 --> 00:13:19.770
of the same print.

00:13:19.770 --> 00:13:24.330
What it depicts is three
snapshots from, let's say,

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:28.740
a life of this
collective organism that

00:13:28.740 --> 00:13:31.020
was created on the
platform Reddit

00:13:31.020 --> 00:13:32.820
as part of this
social experiment

00:13:32.820 --> 00:13:37.230
called Place, which took place
soon after our experiment

00:13:37.230 --> 00:13:41.100
with Adam and others.

00:13:41.100 --> 00:13:43.200
And so basically,
this experiment

00:13:43.200 --> 00:13:47.220
consisted in over one
million people who were--

00:13:47.220 --> 00:13:49.260
whoever wanted to
join Reddit platform

00:13:49.260 --> 00:13:51.030
at this moment and
this project, they

00:13:51.030 --> 00:13:55.230
could put a single pixel in a
chosen color on a white canvas.

00:13:55.230 --> 00:13:57.540
So you can actually see the
white in the background.

00:13:57.540 --> 00:14:01.180
And people started collaborating
and using various channels

00:14:01.180 --> 00:14:05.520
to collaborate on various
collectively built forms,

00:14:05.520 --> 00:14:08.220
from portraits of politicians
that you can see here,

00:14:08.220 --> 00:14:12.060
from flags and, like, the
European Union flag, Puerto

00:14:12.060 --> 00:14:13.440
Rican flag, and so on.

00:14:13.440 --> 00:14:16.680
Artworks-- here we can see a
very small fragment on the Mona

00:14:16.680 --> 00:14:17.610
Lisa--

00:14:17.610 --> 00:14:20.800
to various kind of slogans of
protest movements and so on.

00:14:20.800 --> 00:14:23.330
And at some point, a very
interesting form emerged.

00:14:23.330 --> 00:14:27.570
This form was later
named the black void.

00:14:27.570 --> 00:14:31.050
And this is this black
kind of-- and this form

00:14:31.050 --> 00:14:32.620
changed over time.

00:14:32.620 --> 00:14:34.290
And you can see here
how it's evolving.

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:36.930
And it looks like a
slime mold or a virus

00:14:36.930 --> 00:14:38.820
or another kind of
collective organism.

00:14:38.820 --> 00:14:41.710
And of course, today with
what's happening in the world,

00:14:41.710 --> 00:14:43.710
and when we're looking
at the pandemic

00:14:43.710 --> 00:14:45.690
and how people around
the world are trying

00:14:45.690 --> 00:14:49.340
to define what this pandemic
is, what this virus is, it

00:14:49.340 --> 00:14:52.110
itself becomes a kind of
collective Rorschach test

00:14:52.110 --> 00:14:55.250
where everybody recognizes
something else in it.

00:14:55.250 --> 00:14:57.570
And it collectively--
it constantly evolves.

00:14:57.570 --> 00:14:59.700
This is the second
very big project

00:14:59.700 --> 00:15:02.160
that I've been recently
developing and presenting.

00:15:02.160 --> 00:15:05.430
And I will say only a
few words very briefly.

00:15:05.430 --> 00:15:07.830
This series of works
is called conversions.

00:15:07.830 --> 00:15:10.080
And it's directly connected
to the experiments

00:15:10.080 --> 00:15:11.760
that we have been
doing with Adam.

00:15:11.760 --> 00:15:17.280
And I continued working with
Agnes Cameron on this project.

00:15:17.280 --> 00:15:18.750
So these are paintings.

00:15:18.750 --> 00:15:21.270
They are liquid
crystal paintings.

00:15:21.270 --> 00:15:24.840
So they are executed
on a copper plate

00:15:24.840 --> 00:15:29.220
with paint that contains
particles of liquid crystals.

00:15:29.220 --> 00:15:33.090
And these paintings
are AI paintings.

00:15:33.090 --> 00:15:36.840
They are powered by the changes
in the artificial society

00:15:36.840 --> 00:15:37.680
model.

00:15:37.680 --> 00:15:40.890
And these models, like in the
experiments we did together,

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:46.050
are based on constant parsing of
the protest movements' Twitter

00:15:46.050 --> 00:15:48.810
feeds, members of the protest
movements' Twitter feeds.

00:15:48.810 --> 00:15:53.160
And these changes
cause differences

00:15:53.160 --> 00:15:55.110
in artificial society model.

00:15:55.110 --> 00:15:58.560
And this, in turn,
causes the delivery

00:15:58.560 --> 00:16:03.060
of various electrical charges
and temperature changes

00:16:03.060 --> 00:16:04.600
to the back of this painting.

00:16:04.600 --> 00:16:06.970
So this is how the
painting changes over time.

00:16:06.970 --> 00:16:09.810
You can see how it's just a few
minutes later and a few minutes

00:16:09.810 --> 00:16:10.890
later.

00:16:10.890 --> 00:16:15.230
And so it oscillates between
a map, a colony of bacteria,

00:16:15.230 --> 00:16:18.540
geology, a slime mold.

00:16:18.540 --> 00:16:20.190
And it perpetually evolves.

00:16:20.190 --> 00:16:23.640
So these are paintings
that, like my earlier works,

00:16:23.640 --> 00:16:25.020
are collectively authored.

00:16:25.020 --> 00:16:27.750
So the authorship is obviously
hybridized and complex.

00:16:27.750 --> 00:16:33.330
But also, they are, in a
way, mining social energies.

00:16:33.330 --> 00:16:34.710
And they always evolve.

00:16:34.710 --> 00:16:36.240
They are like living organisms.

00:16:36.240 --> 00:16:38.940
And last but not least, I
wanted to mention something

00:16:38.940 --> 00:16:40.410
very important
for both the works

00:16:40.410 --> 00:16:43.980
that we did with Boris
Katz and his students,

00:16:43.980 --> 00:16:45.600
and also for these
works, and which

00:16:45.600 --> 00:16:48.510
remains a very important
issue for me, is

00:16:48.510 --> 00:16:53.790
that the proceeds from
the sales of these works,

00:16:53.790 --> 00:16:56.652
if they ever get sold,
is always redistributed.

00:16:56.652 --> 00:16:58.860
In the case of the works
that you are now looking at,

00:16:58.860 --> 00:17:02.160
the conversions, I give back
a portion of the profits

00:17:02.160 --> 00:17:04.140
to the protest movements.

00:17:04.140 --> 00:17:10.380
In the case of the works
that we did with Boris Katz,

00:17:10.380 --> 00:17:13.410
the proceeds from these
works are redistributed even

00:17:13.410 --> 00:17:16.380
among the workers
of Amazon Mechanical

00:17:16.380 --> 00:17:18.280
Turk that participated in it.

00:17:18.280 --> 00:17:21.119
And so I'm interested in
continuing the exploration

00:17:21.119 --> 00:17:23.700
of redistribution, how
redistribution of capital

00:17:23.700 --> 00:17:27.540
can be explored with
artificial intelligence

00:17:27.540 --> 00:17:30.900
and how we can today
think about the crossover

00:17:30.900 --> 00:17:33.990
of collective intelligence
and artificial intelligence

00:17:33.990 --> 00:17:35.700
in the arts.

00:17:35.700 --> 00:17:37.870
Cool.

00:17:37.870 --> 00:17:41.130
I think that it's almost
time for us to sign off.

00:17:41.130 --> 00:17:45.090
But I just wanted to say that I
think the project and speaking

00:17:45.090 --> 00:17:51.540
with you, Agnieszka, for me was
a real onset of using hybrids

00:17:51.540 --> 00:17:54.210
outside to think
about hybrids inside.

00:17:54.210 --> 00:17:57.360
I'm so excited about
the idea that we

00:17:57.360 --> 00:17:59.730
are many more intelligences
than we give us

00:17:59.730 --> 00:18:00.870
credit for ourselves.

00:18:00.870 --> 00:18:03.852
And we can think about that
in terms of the gut brain

00:18:03.852 --> 00:18:05.310
or we can think
about that in terms

00:18:05.310 --> 00:18:07.002
of the intelligence
in our left foot

00:18:07.002 --> 00:18:08.460
or we can think
about that in terms

00:18:08.460 --> 00:18:10.200
of biological neural networks.

00:18:10.200 --> 00:18:14.160
But this idea of your work
showing these outside hybrids

00:18:14.160 --> 00:18:17.010
taking us and putting us in
touch with our hybrids inside,

00:18:17.010 --> 00:18:19.650
and also how that
demands that we then

00:18:19.650 --> 00:18:22.710
think about collective
authorship and redistribution

00:18:22.710 --> 00:18:26.970
of benefits from what it is that
we make as hybrid organisms.

00:18:26.970 --> 00:18:29.220
But I think it's
time to sign off.

00:18:29.220 --> 00:18:34.148
I just want to give a big thank
you to CAST for this series,

00:18:34.148 --> 00:18:35.940
but also for the
opportunity to collaborate

00:18:35.940 --> 00:18:39.610
with Agnieszka, which has been
awesome, and will continue.

00:18:39.610 --> 00:18:41.520
And that's about it.

00:18:41.520 --> 00:18:42.690
Thank you so much.

00:18:42.690 --> 00:18:44.070
Thank you-- thank you.

00:18:44.070 --> 00:18:46.140
I would like to give
my special thank you

00:18:46.140 --> 00:18:47.790
to Leila Kinney, who
was the person who

00:18:47.790 --> 00:18:52.200
invited me first to join CAST.

00:18:52.200 --> 00:18:53.747
Thank you Katherine Higgins.

00:18:53.747 --> 00:18:54.580
Thank you, everyone.

00:18:54.580 --> 00:18:55.510
Thank you, Adam.

00:18:55.510 --> 00:18:56.790
It was a pleasure.

00:18:56.790 --> 00:18:57.630
Right on.

00:18:57.630 --> 00:18:58.440
Thanks, y'all.

00:18:58.440 --> 00:18:59.730
Be well.

00:18:59.730 --> 00:19:02.370
Thank you all for joining
us for this conversation

00:19:02.370 --> 00:19:05.220
with Agnieszka Kurant
and Adam Haar Horowitz.

